WEBVTT

0
00:00.150 --> 00:01.330
<v Krista>In this video, we're talking about</v>

1
00:01.330 --> 00:03.500
improper fractions and mixed numbers.

2
00:03.500 --> 00:05.580
And in order to explain these concepts,

3
00:05.580 --> 00:07.560
we're going to talk a lot about pie.

4
00:07.560 --> 00:09.890
Our first example is going to be about strawberry pie.

5
00:09.890 --> 00:12.430
Our second example is going to be about apple pie.

6
00:12.430 --> 00:13.490
So what we want to do is,

7
00:13.490 --> 00:15.290
we've been given here a certain amount of pie,

8
00:15.290 --> 00:17.660
and we want to express the amount of pie that we have

9
00:17.660 --> 00:20.470
as both a mixed number and as an improper fraction.

10
00:20.470 --> 00:22.020
So if I just look at this here,

11
00:22.020 --> 00:23.880
and I pretend that these are four pies, right,

12
00:23.880 --> 00:25.780
one, two, three, four pies.

13
00:25.780 --> 00:27.890
And I want to say, how much pie do I have?

14
00:27.890 --> 00:30.330
Well, half of this pie has already been eaten.

15
00:30.330 --> 00:32.350
This fourth pie, half of it's been eaten.

16
00:32.350 --> 00:33.640
How much pie do I have left?

17
00:33.640 --> 00:36.450
Well, logically I can just count how much I have left.

18
00:36.450 --> 00:39.410
I can say I have one, two, three whole pies,

19
00:39.410 --> 00:41.930
and I have half of this pie remaining,

20
00:41.930 --> 00:43.670
so I have 3 1/2 pies left.

21
00:43.670 --> 00:46.160
But how do I express that mathematically?

22
00:46.160 --> 00:49.560
Well, it's really easy to express this as a mixed number.

23
00:49.560 --> 00:51.810
We can say that we have 3 1/2 pies left.

24
00:51.810 --> 00:53.520
If we want to write this as a mixed number,

25
00:53.520 --> 00:57.230
we just call it 3 1/2, and we write it this way.

26
00:57.230 --> 00:58.870
Three here represents the number

27
00:58.870 --> 01:00.270
of whole pies that we have left.

28
01:00.270 --> 01:01.200
It's a whole number.

29
01:01.200 --> 01:04.100
And 1/2 represents the amount we have remaining

30
01:04.100 --> 01:05.740
of the pie that's been partially eaten.

31
01:05.740 --> 01:08.860
So we have three pies, plus we have a half a pie,

32
01:08.860 --> 01:10.590
so we have 3 1/2 pies.

33
01:10.590 --> 01:13.180
And we write it this way, a whole number and a fraction,

34
01:13.180 --> 01:15.780
and it really means three plus 1/2.

35
01:15.780 --> 01:17.560
We have 3 1/2 pies.

36
01:17.560 --> 01:19.040
Now I can also express the amount

37
01:19.040 --> 01:21.470
of pie I have as an improper fraction.

38
01:21.470 --> 01:24.410
An improper fraction is a single fraction where

39
01:24.410 --> 01:28.220
the numerator is greater than or equal to the denominator.

40
01:28.220 --> 01:30.250
So instead of writing 3 1/2,

41
01:30.250 --> 01:32.360
three wholes and a half remaining,

42
01:32.360 --> 01:34.080
I can write this as one fraction,

43
01:34.080 --> 01:35.290
and the way that I do that is

44
01:35.290 --> 01:38.270
by looking at the number of halves that I have.

45
01:38.270 --> 01:40.050
So instead of three wholes and a half,

46
01:40.050 --> 01:42.400
I can say, how many halves do I have left?

47
01:42.400 --> 01:44.940
Well, I have two halves in this first pie, right?

48
01:44.940 --> 01:46.860
One, two halves makes a whole.

49
01:46.860 --> 01:51.550
So I have one, two, three, four, five, six, and seven.

50
01:51.550 --> 01:54.480
I have seven halves of pie remaining.

51
01:54.480 --> 01:56.130
So how do I write seven halves?

52
01:56.130 --> 01:58.430
Well, the way that I write it is like this.

53
01:58.430 --> 02:02.490
I say I have seven times 1/2 pies remaining.

54
02:02.490 --> 02:03.990
So I have half a pie.

55
02:03.990 --> 02:05.520
I have seven of them.

56
02:05.520 --> 02:08.250
When I multiply, I get 7/2.

57
02:08.250 --> 02:09.280
So I can write it this way.

58
02:09.280 --> 02:11.430
I have 7/2 pie remaining.

59
02:11.430 --> 02:13.210
Now the important thing to realize

60
02:13.210 --> 02:16.320
is that we used 7/2 and 3 1/2

61
02:16.320 --> 02:20.290
to express the same amount, which means that 3 1/2 and 7/2

62
02:20.290 --> 02:21.840
have to be equal to each other.

63
02:21.840 --> 02:23.940
So there's got to be some way to convert

64
02:23.940 --> 02:27.230
between mixed numbers and improper fractions and vice-versa.

65
02:27.230 --> 02:28.860
And in fact, there is.

66
02:28.860 --> 02:32.630
If we have a mixed number, 3 1/2, we can convert it

67
02:32.630 --> 02:35.360
to an improper fraction using the following process,

68
02:35.360 --> 02:37.000
and this works every single time.

69
02:37.000 --> 02:39.230
We take the denominator of the fraction

70
02:39.230 --> 02:40.840
in the mixed number, so that's two.

71
02:40.840 --> 02:42.890
We take two, and we multiply it by the whole number.

72
02:42.890 --> 02:44.930
So two times three is six.

73
02:44.930 --> 02:46.820
Then to that result, we add the numerator,

74
02:46.820 --> 02:48.240
which, in this case, is one.

75
02:48.240 --> 02:49.970
Six plus one is seven.

76
02:49.970 --> 02:51.280
So then we have seven.

77
02:51.280 --> 02:53.120
We take that number, that result,

78
02:53.120 --> 02:55.490
and we divide it by the original denominator,

79
02:55.490 --> 02:57.520
which is two, and we get 7/2.

80
02:57.520 --> 03:00.340
So three times two is six, six plus one is seven,

81
03:00.340 --> 03:04.640
seven over the original denominator is equal to 7/2.

82
03:04.640 --> 03:06.730
So that's how you convert from a mixed number

83
03:06.730 --> 03:09.310
to an improper fraction, and that'll work every time.

84
03:09.310 --> 03:10.570
You can follow that process.

85
03:10.570 --> 03:11.610
Now what if we want to convert

86
03:11.610 --> 03:14.270
from an improper fraction to a mixed number?

87
03:14.270 --> 03:16.100
Well, this essentially is telling us,

88
03:16.100 --> 03:18.610
remember fractions are ratios that tell us

89
03:18.610 --> 03:21.070
to divide the numerator by the denominator.

90
03:21.070 --> 03:23.300
All we have to do here is perform the division.

91
03:23.300 --> 03:25.540
So we do seven divided by two.

92
03:25.540 --> 03:27.150
So if we just do long division,

93
03:27.150 --> 03:30.260
and we say, how many times does two go into seven?

94
03:30.260 --> 03:31.750
Well, I put my decimal point here.

95
03:31.750 --> 03:32.730
I'll put a zero.

96
03:32.730 --> 03:34.600
I'll bring my decimal point directly up.

97
03:34.600 --> 03:36.560
Two goes into seven three times.

98
03:36.560 --> 03:38.350
Three times two is six.

99
03:38.350 --> 03:40.310
Seven minus six is one.

100
03:40.310 --> 03:42.690
Then I bring down my zero here.

101
03:42.690 --> 03:44.970
And two goes into 10 five times.

102
03:44.970 --> 03:47.770
So I get 3.5, or 3 1/2, right?

103
03:47.770 --> 03:49.970
3.5 is equal to 3 1/2.

104
03:49.970 --> 03:52.840
And so I get back here to my mixed number.

105
03:52.840 --> 03:54.450
So if you have an improper fraction,

106
03:54.450 --> 03:57.160
you can do long division to convert it to a mixed number.

107
03:57.160 --> 03:59.580
If you have a mixed number, you can do that process.

108
03:59.580 --> 04:02.480
Denominator times whole number plus numerator,

109
04:02.480 --> 04:03.960
all over the original denominator

110
04:03.960 --> 04:05.800
gets you to the improper fraction,

111
04:05.800 --> 04:07.910
and these are both completely valid ways

112
04:07.910 --> 04:09.810
to express a real amount of something.

113
04:09.810 --> 04:11.600
So we had 3 1/2 pies.

114
04:11.600 --> 04:13.940
We can write it as 3 1/2, a mixed number,

115
04:13.940 --> 04:16.910
or as 7/2, an improper fraction.

116
04:16.910 --> 04:18.730
Let's look at a second example here.

117
04:18.730 --> 04:19.740
You can see that we have,

118
04:19.740 --> 04:21.500
we'll pretend that this is apple pie, right?

119
04:21.500 --> 04:23.640
So we have four pies.

120
04:23.640 --> 04:25.530
One of them has been partially eaten,

121
04:25.530 --> 04:27.630
and it looks like a third of it has been eaten.

122
04:27.630 --> 04:29.240
So this pie that's been partially eaten,

123
04:29.240 --> 04:31.180
you can see it's been divided into three parts,

124
04:31.180 --> 04:34.290
one, two, three parts, and one of them has been eaten.

125
04:34.290 --> 04:36.470
So 1/3 of this pie has been eaten.

126
04:36.470 --> 04:38.520
I have 2/3 of it remaining.

127
04:38.520 --> 04:41.500
So how much do I have if I want to say a mixed number?

128
04:41.500 --> 04:42.690
Well, I know based on this

129
04:42.690 --> 04:45.330
partially eaten pie, that I have 2/3 here.

130
04:45.330 --> 04:49.610
So if I say one whole pie, two whole pies, three whole pies,

131
04:49.610 --> 04:54.610
and 2/3 of a pie, then I can say 3 2/3

132
04:54.960 --> 04:58.290
is what I have left in terms of a mixed number.

133
04:58.290 --> 04:59.500
So what do I do then if I want to

134
04:59.500 --> 05:01.510
express this as an improper fraction?

135
05:01.510 --> 05:03.830
Well, I look at my pie that's been partially eaten.

136
05:03.830 --> 05:06.100
I know a third has been eaten, I have 2/3 left.

137
05:06.100 --> 05:07.660
So because I'm dealing with thirds,

138
05:07.660 --> 05:09.060
I want to count in thirds.

139
05:09.060 --> 05:10.710
So if I look at a whole pie,

140
05:10.710 --> 05:13.750
I know that one whole pie is going to be 3/3, right?

141
05:13.750 --> 05:15.500
Three over three is equal to one,

142
05:15.500 --> 05:17.500
so I'm going to have 3/3 in one whole pie.

143
05:17.500 --> 05:20.670
So I can say one, two, three, four, five, six,

144
05:20.670 --> 05:23.630
seven, eight, nine, 10, 11.

145
05:23.630 --> 05:27.600
So I know I'm going to have 11/3 as my improper fraction.

146
05:27.600 --> 05:28.780
If I didn't want to count

147
05:28.780 --> 05:30.750
and instead I wanted to use my mixed number

148
05:30.750 --> 05:32.800
and convert that to an improper fraction,

149
05:32.800 --> 05:36.430
I just do three times three is nine, nine plus two is 11,

150
05:36.430 --> 05:40.840
and 11 over my original denominator of three gives me 11/3.

151
05:40.840 --> 05:44.077
If instead I had started with an improper fraction, 11/3,

152
05:44.077 --> 05:46.510
and I wanted to convert that to a mixed number,

153
05:46.510 --> 05:49.290
I just divide three into 11.

154
05:49.290 --> 05:52.940
I put my decimal point here, bring it up into my answer.

155
05:52.940 --> 05:55.130
Three into 11 is three.

156
05:55.130 --> 05:56.770
Three times three is nine.

157
05:56.770 --> 05:58.780
11 minus nine is two.

158
05:58.780 --> 06:01.510
Because my remainder is less than my divisor,

159
06:01.510 --> 06:03.730
two is less than three, I know I'm done.

160
06:03.730 --> 06:05.030
And I use this as my remainder,

161
06:05.030 --> 06:10.030
so I have three and then two over three left, so 3 2/3,

162
06:10.440 --> 06:13.260
which gets me back to my mixed number answer.

163
06:13.260 --> 06:14.560
So either way, when you're dealing

164
06:14.560 --> 06:16.580
with a real amount like this, you can express it

165
06:16.580 --> 06:18.800
as a mixed number or an improper fraction.

166
06:18.800 --> 06:20.860
And you can convert easily between the two,

167
06:20.860 --> 06:22.900
either with long division to go from

168
06:22.900 --> 06:25.000
an improper fraction to a mixed number,

169
06:25.000 --> 06:27.220
or with that process where we take denominator

170
06:27.220 --> 06:29.550
times whole number plus the numerator

171
06:29.550 --> 06:32.010
and then divided by the original denominator

172
06:32.010 --> 06:34.560
to get from a mixed number to an improper fraction.